<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-wrangling">
  <title>Wrangling</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "He did not arrive at this conclusion by the decent process of quiet, logical deduction, nor yet by the
        blinding flash of glorious intuition, but by the shoddy, untidy process halfway between the two by which one
        usually gets to know things."
      </p>
      <p>--- Margery Allingham, <em>Death of a Ghost</em></p>
    </blockquote>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-wrang-what-why">
    <title>Data wrangling: what and why</title>

    <p>
      Data wrangling covers cleaning, organizing, and restructuring data for analysis. Real datasets almost never
      arrive in analysis-ready form and typically require iterative checking, correction, transformation, and
      aggregation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-wrang-clean-transform">
    <title>Data cleaning and transforming</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wrang-errors">
      <title>Errors, typos, outliers, missing values</title>
      <p>
        Errors can be obvious, emerge from graphics, or appear only after deeper analysis. Missing values may be coded
        inconsistently and require explicit recoding before reliable work is possible.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wrang-transform-combine">
      <title>Transforming and combining variables</title>
      <p>
        Transformations can expose structure and improve comparability; combining variables can reveal relationships
        not visible in single-variable views. Standardization is especially important for multi-variable displays like
        parallel coordinate plots.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-flighttx">
        <image source="images/flighttX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Spaceflight mission times on linear and log scales.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-olyaa">
        <image source="images/olyAA-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Percentage differences in Olympic gold-medal performances versus recent baselines.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-newcty">
        <image source="images/newctY-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Newcomb speed-of-light estimates by sequence (left) and by date (right).</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-wrang-restructure">
    <title>Restructuring: subsetting, grouping, aggregating</title>

    <p>
      Subsetting, grouping, and aggregation generate different analytical views and summaries. Hierarchical structures
      are manageable, but multiple cross-cutting factors create many valid aggregation choices.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-comcatsx">
      <image source="images/comCatsX-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Comrades Marathon finishers by age group and sex over time.</p></caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-wrang-naming">
    <title>Naming, renaming and reclassifying</title>

    <p>
      Wrangling creates many intermediate datasets and derived variables. Clear naming, explicit recoding, and careful
      type handling reduce mistakes and make workflows easier to audit and maintain.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-wrang-layers">
    <title>Wrangling for layered graphics</title>

    <p>
      Layered graphics benefit from explicit data preparation for each layer. Different layers can use full data,
      subsets, summaries, models, or related datasets, provided scales are aligned.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-histlayers">
      <image source="images/histlayers-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Layer-by-layer build-up of a histogram with density overlays.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-histlayers2">
      <image source="images/histlayers2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Histogram with two superimposed density estimates.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Layer order matters: background and guides first, then data and model summaries, then annotations. Transparency,
      line weight, and ordering all affect interpretability.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-socdfx">
      <image source="images/socDfX-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Draw-rate series for top football tiers in England, Spain, and Italy.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-astgy">
      <image source="images/astgY-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Age by mission year with smooths and confidence intervals.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flightx">
      <image source="images/flightX-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Logged spaceflight mission times by year and nationality.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wrang-wormchart-layers">
      <title>The layers of a league wormchart</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-worm54">
        <image source="images/worm54-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Wormchart for the 1954-55 English League Championship season.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-wormlayers">
        <image source="images/wormlayers-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Layer-by-layer construction of the 1954-55 wormchart.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-wrang-merge-repro">
    <title>Putting datasets together and reproducibility</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wrang-reading-merging">
      <title>Reading in and merging datasets</title>
      <p>
        Real projects often combine multiple files and formats. Merging requires robust keys, harmonized codes, and
        consistent definitions across sources.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wrang-reproducibility">
      <title>Reproducibility</title>
      <p>
        Reproducibility is strongest when both raw data and full wrangling/analysis code are available. Reporting
        should clearly document reconstruction steps, assumptions, and omissions.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-wrang-main-points">
      <title>Main points</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Data cleaning continues throughout an investigation, not just at the start.</p></li>
        <li><p>Subsetting, grouping, and aggregation create many valid analytical views.</p></li>
        <li><p>Layered data structures support clear and flexible graphic construction.</p></li>
        <li><p>Wrangling is an essential foundation for trustworthy graphical analysis.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
